  "answer_text": "Making comfortable, respectful eye contact with someone who has a \"lazy eye\" (medically referred to as **strabismus**, where the eyes are misaligned and point in different directions) is a very common concern. People with strabismus are often highly aware of their condition and can feel self-conscious when they notice someone struggling to figure out where to look. \n\nTo make the interaction natural and comfortable for both of you, use the following practical strategies:\n\n### 1. Look at the \"dominant\" eye\nUsually, a person with strabismus is actively focusing and looking at you with one eye (their dominant eye), while the other eye drifts inward, outward, or upward. \n* **What to do:** Simply look at the eye that is making direct, straight contact with you. Treat that as their primary eye and maintain your gaze there.\n\n### 2. Use the \"bridge of the nose\" trick\nIf you find it difficult to tell which eye is dominant, or if you feel distracted, this is the most effective and widely recommended strategy.\n* **What to do:** Focus your gaze on the bridge of their nose or the space right between their eyebrows. \n* **Why it works:** To the other person, looking at the bridge of their nose feels exactly like normal, direct eye contact. It removes all guesswork for you and ensures they feel fully engaged and listened to.\n\n### 3. Pick one eye and stick to it\nIf you decide to look at their eyes, choose just one eye and keep your focus there. \n* **Why it works:** In any normal conversation, we rarely look at both of a person's eyes simultaneously; we naturally drift to one eye or the other. Picking one eye and sticking with it simulates normal eye contact. \n* *Note:* Some people with strabismus \"alternate\" which eye is dominant mid-conversation. If they switch, trying to follow their gaze can make your eyes dart back and forth. If this happens, transitioning your gaze to the bridge of their nose is your best option.\n\n### 4. Practice \"soft focus\" on their whole face\nAvoid laser-focusing intensely on their eyes. Instead, take in their entire face\u2014their mouth, expressions, and gestures\u2014using a relaxed, \"soft\" focus. This is how most people naturally converse when they are comfortable with each other. \n\n---\n\n### What to Avoid\n* **Don't do the \"ping-pong\" gaze:** Avoid darting your eyes back and forth between their two eyes trying to figure out which one is looking at you. Rapidly shifting your gaze is highly noticeable and can make the other person feel very self-conscious.\n* **Don't stare at the drifting eye:** It can be naturally tempting to look at the eye that is turned because of human curiosity, but try to actively resist this. \n* **Don't look away entirely:** Looking at their ears, the floor, or over their shoulder to avoid the situation can make you seem disengaged, bored, or uncomfortable. \n* **Don't mention it:** Unless they bring it up first, never point out or ask about their eye. \n\nUltimately, remember that **active listening**\u2014nodding, smiling, and responding warmly\u2014is much more important to a successful conversation than perfect eye alignment. If you focus on what they are saying rather than how they are looking at you, the interaction will naturally feel comfortable for both of you.",
